## Incremental rendering: hoist embedded images out of the per-render SVG

Problem: a page with a huge embedded raster image lags on every keystroke, even
though compile is incremental. typst_svg's base64 encoding is already comemo-cached,
so the repeat cost is downstream: the inline base64 is rebuilt into the page SVG,
the multi-MB string is structured-cloned across the worker->main boundary, and the
browser re-parses + re-decodes the image on innerHTML. Per-page hashing does not help
(editing text on the page changes the page).

Fix (JS only, no Rust change needed): hoist images in typsten-worker.ts.
- After render_page, scan the SVG for inline `xlink:href="data:image/...;base64,..."`,
  replace each with a stable ref (e.g. `xlink:href="typst-img:<hash>"`), and collect
  { hash -> bytes }. Dedup against a set of hashes already sent so an unchanged image
  is not re-sent.
- Return { svg (refs only), newImages }; send image bytes as zero-copy transferables
  (Comlink transfers ArrayBuffers without cloning).
- On main: keep a hash -> blobURL cache (URL.createObjectURL), rewrite refs to blob
  URLs, then insert. Editing text re-sends only the small text SVG; the image crosses
  the boundary once and is reused as a stable blob URL (no re-transfer, no re-decode).

To fully avoid the browser re-decoding, update pages as their own DOM nodes instead of
blowing away innerHTML (pairs with per-page-node rendering).

Escalation (only if the inline-base64 SVG reaching the worker is still too big): have
Rust emit image refs instead of inlining. typst_svg inlines unconditionally, so this is
a custom render_image path (fork, or upstream a hook).
